assessments:
  - discriminant: mcq
    value:
      id: 512-mcq-01
      prompt: A small online business uses software to track stock, update prices, and schedule courier collections from its warehouse. Which functional area of the business does this most directly support?
      options:
        - Human Resources
        - Marketing
        - Logistics
        - Finance
      correct_index: 2
      feedback_correct: |
        Correct. Stock tracking, pricing updates, and courier scheduling
        are all logistics activities: getting the right goods to the
        right customer at the right time.
      feedback_incorrect: |
        The answer is "Logistics". Logistics covers the movement and
        storage of goods, including inventory tracking and dispatch.
        Marketing would cover promotion and customer acquisition;
        Finance would cover pricing policy and accounting; HR covers
        people, not stock.
      difficulty: foundation
  - discriminant: nei
    value:
      id: 512-nei-01
      stimulus: |
        Amaya is 17 and has been running a Vinted shop for eighteen
        months, selling second-hand clothes she sources at car boot
        sales and charity shops. Her shop has grown past 400 items
        listed at any one time and she's finding it hard to keep on
        top of it. She is considering whether to invest time in
        better IT tools, specifically around how she photographs,
        lists, and dispatches items (logistics) and how she promotes
        her shop to new buyers (marketing).
      question: |
        Identify two digital tools Amaya could use, one for logistics
        and one for marketing, and explain how each would support the
        specific function.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          One valid logistics tool (examples that would score: a barcode
          or QR scanner app for tracking items, a spreadsheet or light
          inventory app for stock records, a courier integration such
          as Royal Mail Click and Drop or InPost app, Vinted's own
          built-in shipping label tool) AND one valid marketing tool
          (examples that would score: Instagram or TikTok for showcasing
          items, Canva for producing listing photos or brand content,
          Vinted's own promoted listings, email or push notifications
          to existing buyers). A student who names two tools from the
          same category can still score partial credit on this mark if
          both tools are well-chosen and clearly named, but a fully
          credited answer identifies one tool from each of the two
          requested categories.
        explain_expected: |
          For the logistics tool: a correct statement of what the tool
          does in relation to the movement or tracking of stock. For
          example, a stock spreadsheet lets Amaya see which items she
          still has, which have sold, and which she has duplicates of,
          so she does not accept a sale she cannot fulfil. A courier
          integration produces postage labels automatically from sale
          data so she does not have to re-enter buyer addresses.
          For the marketing tool: a correct statement of what the tool
          does in relation to acquiring or retaining buyers. For example,
          Instagram lets her post photos of new stock to an audience
          that has already chosen to follow her, so she reaches buyers
          without paying for exposure on Vinted itself. Canva helps her
          produce visually consistent listing photos that make her shop
          look more professional than a one-off seller.
        impact_expected: |
          The concrete consequence for Amaya's shop must be described,
          not just implied. Examples: fewer cancelled orders because
          stock is accurately tracked, faster dispatch times because
          labels are produced in one click rather than re-typed, more
          repeat buyers because her Instagram keeps her in their feed
          between purchases, higher listing-to-sale conversion because
          photos are consistent and attractive. The impact should link
          back to the specific problem in the scenario (400+ items,
          hard to keep on top of, wanting new buyers), not be generic.
      exemplar_answer: |
        For logistics, Amaya could use a stock spreadsheet. This would
        let her record each item with a unique reference, where she is
        storing it, and whether it has sold, so she can check her real
        stock position against her 400+ Vinted listings and avoid
        accepting a sale for an item she has already sold elsewhere.
        This fixes the specific problem of losing track at scale: she
        would dispatch faster and avoid the reputation damage of
        cancelling orders.

        For marketing, she could use Instagram to post photos of new
        stock to followers. Unlike Vinted's internal feed, Instagram
        reaches buyers who have already shown interest in her shop,
        and she can link back to the Vinted listing directly. This
        addresses her goal of finding new buyers without depending
        entirely on Vinted's own discovery algorithm, and building a
        following means the next batch of items has a ready audience
        rather than starting from zero.
